{
  "requirement": "When `habeas status` or `habeas status --all` displays a Markdown table, it MUST left-align and space-pad the Definition and Evidence columns so every row, including the header and separator row, has its `|` column delimiters in the same positions. Column widths MUST be determined by the widest displayed cell in each column.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The implementation is marked and the test covers it. I found no gaps.\n\n**Implementation.** `render_evidence_table` in `src/markdown.rs` is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.cli.status_table_alignment`.\n- It computes each column width as the maximum of the cell lengths in that column and the header word (`Definition` or `Evidence`). That gives widths set by the widest displayed cell, header included.\n- It writes the header, the `---` separator and every data row with the same format string, `| {x:<dw$} | {y:<ew$} |`. Every row therefore has left-aligned, space-padded cells and `|` delimiters in the same positions.\n- `src/commands.rs` (lines 93–96) calls this function for both `habeas status` and `habeas status --all`. The `all` flag only changes which rows are collected, so one code path serves both commands.\n- Using `len()` (bytes) for width could over-widen a column if a cell had non-ASCII characters. In practice the cells are ASCII. Definition names pass `valid_name`, which allows only ASCII alphanumerics, `_`, `-` and `.`. The cell text also contains backticks, parentheses and the kind noun, and the evidence labels are plain words.\n- The marking is complete. The only other users of the function, `src/index.rs` and the call site in `src/commands.rs`, are marked for `shared_status_rendering`, and the alignment logic lives only in this function.\n\n**Test.** `status_tables_align_each_column_to_its_widest_cell` in `tests/commands.rs` is marked `{test}`. It runs both `status` and `status --all` and extracts the table lines. It then checks that:\n- the table has more than two lines;\n- every line has `|` at the same byte positions as the header line, so the header and separator rows are covered too;\n- each cell begins with exactly one space of padding and ends with a space, so cells are left-aligned;\n- each column's width equals the widest trimmed cell in that column, which covers the \"widest displayed cell\" rule;\n- the separator row contains `---` in both columns.\n\nThe test is behavioural and covers both commands. The design space is small, so I judge it sufficient.",
